ERR1 = "Use the full library-unit name instead of this obsolescent short renaming"
ERR2 = "Avoid the obsolescent 'Constrained attribute on an object of a private type"
ERR3 = "Avoid using the obsolescent predefined package ASCII"
ERR4 = "Avoid using the obsolescent exception Numeric_Error"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_7.1.1",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_7.1.1": "Published Standards/Ada 95/7.1.1 Obsolescent Features",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_7.1.1": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Portability",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_7.1.1": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; In programs or components intended to have a long life, avoid
using the features of Ada declared as "obsolescent" by Annex J of the
Ada Reference Manual (1995), unless the use of the feature is needed for
backward compatibility with Ada 83 (Ada Reference Manual 1983). Document
the use of any obsolescent features.</p>
<p>&#8226; Avoid using the following features:</p>
<ul>
<li>The short renamings of the packages in the predefined environment
(e.g., Text_IO as opposed to Ada.Text_IO)</li>
<li>The character replacements of ! for |, : for #, and % for
quotation marks</li>
<li>Reduced accuracy subtypes of floating-point types</li>
<li>The 'Constrained attribute as applied to private types</li>
<li>The predefined package ASCII</li>
<li>The exception Numeric_Error</li>
<li>Various representation specifications, including at clauses, mod
clauses, interrupt entries, and the Storage_Size attribute</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>Ten years of reflection on the use of Ada 83 led to the conclusion that
some features of the original language are not as useful as originally
intended. These features have been replaced with others in the Ada 95
revision. It would have been desirable to remove the obsolescent
features completely, but that would have prevented the upward compatible
transition of programs from Ada 83 to Ada 95. Thus, the obsolescent
features remain in the language and are explicitly labeled as such in
Annex J of the Ada Reference Manual (1995). The features listed in Annex
J are candidates for removal from the language during its next revision.
If a program's lifetime may extend beyond the next language revision, it
should avoid the obsolescent language features unless backward
compatibility with Ada 83 forces their use.</p>

<p><b>Exceptions</b></p>
<p>When you instantiate Ada.Text_IO.Float_IO, the values of the
Default_Fore and Default_Aft fields are set from the values of the 'Fore
and 'Aft attributes of the actual floating-point type used in the
instantiation. If you declare a reduced accuracy floating-point type
that you then use to instantiate Ada.Text_IO.Float_IO, the output field
widths are determined from the reduced accuracy type, although the
implementation accuracy is unchanged (Rationale 1995, &sect;3.3).</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>Four of the seven listed features are automated: the short predefined-
package renamings (<code>Text_IO</code>, <code>Sequential_IO</code>, <code>Direct_IO</code>,
<code>IO_Exceptions</code>, <code>Calendar</code>, <code>Unchecked_Conversion</code>,
<code>Unchecked_Deallocation</code>), <code>'Constrained</code> applied to a private
type, the predefined package <code>ASCII</code>, and the exception
<code>Numeric_Error</code>.</p>
<p>Not automated: the character replacements (<code>!</code>/<code>:</code>/<code>%</code>) and the
old-style representation clauses (<code>at</code>/<code>mod</code> clauses, interrupt
entries, obsolescent <code>Storage_Size</code>) need more research into whether
and how Understand represents that legacy syntax. Reduced accuracy
floating-point subtypes have no fixed syntax to key off &#8212; just a
target-hardware-dependent judgment call. Whether a use is documented, or
genuinely required for Ada 83 compatibility, is a determination about
intent with no structural signature to check.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


_OBSOLESCENT_RENAMES = (
    "Text_IO",
    "Sequential_IO",
    "Direct_IO",
    "IO_Exceptions",
    "Calendar",
    "Unchecked_Conversion",
    "Unchecked_Deallocation",
)


def _next_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.next()

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.next()

    return lex


def _check_obsolescent_renames(check, file):
    for with_ref in file.filerefs("With"):
        ent = with_ref.ent()

        if str(ent.longname()) in _OBSOLESCENT_RENAMES:
            check.violation(ent, file, with_ref.line(),
                            with_ref.column(), ERR1)


def _check_lexer_based(check, file):
    lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=True)
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if lex.token() == "Identifier" and lex.text().lower() == "numeric_error":
            check.violation(None, file, lex.line_begin(),
                            lex.column_begin(), ERR4)

        if lex.token() == "Identifier" and lex.ent():
            if str(lex.ent().longname()) == "STANDARD.ASCII":
                check.violation(None, file, lex.line_begin(),
                                lex.column_begin(), ERR3)

        if lex.text() == "'":
            attr_lex = _next_significant(lex)

            if attr_lex and attr_lex.text().lower() == "constrained":
                prev = lex.previous(True, True)
                prefix_ent = prev.ent() if prev else None
                type_ref = prefix_ent.ref(
                    "Typed", "Type") if prefix_ent else None

                if type_ref and type_ref.ent().kind().check("Private"):
                    check.violation(
                        prefix_ent, file, lex.line_begin(), lex.column_begin(), ERR2
                    )

        lex = lex.next()


def check(check, file):
    _check_obsolescent_renames(check, file)
    _check_lexer_based(check, file)
